1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,400
Today I want to talk about deliberate practice, because I think we use the word practice to mean almost anything.

2
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:17,520
Most people think practice means repetition. You play the piece again, you hit the shots again, you rewrite the page again.

3
00:00:17,820 --> 00:00:24,960
But repetition on its own mostly makes you more consistent at the level you are already at.

4
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:34,080
The research on expert performance points at something more specific. Experts pick a task slightly beyond what they can do comfortably.

5
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:41,940
Then they get fast, specific feedback on what went wrong, and they change one thing and try again.

6
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:53,1000
I'd say the part that people skip is the feedback. If nobody tells you what went wrong, you cannot adjust, you just repeat your mistake with more confidence.

7
00:00:54,300 --> 00:01:02,280
So how do you get feedback when you are learning alone? A recording is the cheapest answer I know.

8
00:01:02,580 --> 00:01:13,500
Record yourself explaining an idea, or playing a passage, and listen back the same day. You will hear things you could not hear while doing it.

9
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:23,460
The second ingredient is a narrow target. Not get better at writing, but make the first paragraph answer the question in two sentences.

10
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:30,480
When the target is narrow, you can tell in a few minutes whether you hit it.

11
00:01:30,780 --> 00:01:40,860
Third, and this one surprised me, you need to stop before you are exhausted. Quality drops quickly after about an hour of hard focus.

12
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:51,660
Elite violinists in one well known study practiced in sessions of around ninety minutes at most, with a rest in the middle, and they napped.

13
00:01:51,960 --> 00:02:00,360
I think this is the most useful finding for ordinary people. Short, sharp, well aimed sessions beat long, foggy ones.

14
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:10,320
There is a trap on the other side, though. Some people hear all this and wait for the perfect plan before they start.

15
00:02:10,620 --> 00:02:20,700
Plans are cheap. A rough session today with a narrow target and one piece of feedback teaches you more than a week of planning.

16
00:02:20,1000 --> 00:02:34,020
So here is what I do. Every morning I pick one small thing I got wrong yesterday. I write it on a card. I work only on that for forty minutes.

17
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:44,820
Then I record a minute of myself doing it, I listen back on a walk, and I write one sentence about what to change tomorrow.

18
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:58,560
It is not glamorous. But after a few months, the cards pile up, and you can see that the things you got wrong in March are things you do without thinking now.

19
00:02:58,860 --> 00:03:06,840
The last point is about motivation. People ask how to stay motivated. I think that is the wrong question.

20
00:03:07,140 --> 00:03:18,060
Motivation follows progress, not the other way around. If you can see yourself getting better at a small thing every week, you will want to continue.

21
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:25,080
So make progress visible. Keep the cards. Keep the recordings. Look at them once a month.

22
00:03:25,380 --> 00:03:35,460
That's the episode. If you take one thing away, pick one narrow thing, get feedback fast, and stop while you still have energy left.
